-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_nonnegativity_constraint_300a7d36_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which plan $(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ satisfies $\\boldsymbol{x}\\ge0$?","options":["$(0,\\ 2.5,\\ 4)$","$(3,\\ -1,\\ 2)$","$(-2,\\ 4,\\ 6)$","$(2,\\ 1,\\ -0.1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dbba04929c65839d436f75fd","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_nonnegativity_constraint_300a7d36_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A profit model has the machine constraint $2x_1+x_2\\le10$ for output $x_1,x_2$ of two products, but no sign restrictions. Why might it recommend $x_2<0$?","options":["Negative output of 2 frees machine time for more of product 1","It never would, since negative output always earns negative profit","It never would, since the constraint already rules this out","Because negative values make the machine constraint fail"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c16ed8cd686befc0ae6cea45","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_nonnegativity_constraint_300a7d36_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the nonnegativity constraint for an amount produced written $x\\ge0$ rather than $x>0$?","options":["Because $x\\ge0$ and $x>0$ allow exactly the same values","So producing nothing, $x=0$, stays an allowed choice","Because strict inequalities are more precise than $\\ge$","Because $x>0$ would allow negative values close to 0"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ebdcc990ebe092b403fa571e","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_ta